-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 20261101.1~sparky9~0 (2026-11-01)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * README: the edition now ships its own dkms (3.4.4-1+sparky1, at 1001 from its repository, with the overlay sparky-stereo-dkms-compat needs), so choosing NVIDIA's dkms takes a record at 1002, not 990; the pins are unchanged (ORDER 342)
  * Supersedes 20261101~sparky9~0 (ORDER 338), which was not published; its changes are in this version

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 20261101~sparky9~0 (2026-11-01)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * First version: NVIDIA's CUDA repository (Debian 13, amd64, flat), its key and its pins in one package, so the image, the hardware setup, the publication's link gate and check-installable all read the same files. Before, the image (iso-build/apt-setup.sh) and the hardware setup (sparky-stereo-hardware's setup_nvidia) wrote them by hand, and the gate and the checker never saw the pin (ORDER 338, ORDER 329's finding)
  * The pin is narrowed to compute: CUDA by NVIDIA's own patterns (CUDA Installation Guide for Linux 13.4) and the CUDA-X libraries and GPUDirect Storage at 1002, NVIDIA's EGL platform libraries at 1002 (NVIDIA's driver at the locked version needs their NVIDIA versions), and the rest of the repository at 100. Before, every package of the repository stood at 1002 (Package: *), so NVIDIA's rebuilds of Debian's own tools won on every install: dkms 3.4.x over Debian's 3.2.2-1
  * NVIDIA's driver stays with sparky-stereo-nvidia-driver-lock, unchanged; the package depends on it, so the repository never comes without the lock
  * NVIDIA's key at NVIDIA's own path, /usr/share/keyrings/cuda-archive-keyring.gpg, byte for byte the file of NVIDIA's cuda-keyring 1.1-1, which this package conflicts with and replaces: a source left by cuda-keyring names the same key and apt accepts both, while two sources with different Signed-By stop apt
  * The first install takes over the source and the pin the image and the hardware setup wrote, when nobody changed them (preinst), and removes their copy of the key from /etc/apt/keyrings when no source names it any more (postinst)
